# Glossary and Acronyms

**Part:** Reference · **Reading time:** dip in as needed · **Pass 1 overview:** [[P1.1:What MRI measures]], [[P1.5:Speed, artifacts and safety]] · **Refreshers you may want:** none — every entry is written in plain language

MRI is full of jargon and acronyms, which can be intimidating and impede understanding. This notebook has three parts:

1. a **plain-language glossary** (alphabetical; each entry 1–3 sentences with a link to the chapter where it is explained properly),
2. an **acronym table**,
3. a **vendor name table**: Siemens, GE and Philips use different trade names for the same sequences and methods.

For symbols and units see [[Notation:Notation and Symbols]]. Two excellent external resources:

* Radiopaedia — general list of pulse-sequence abbreviations: https://radiopaedia.org/articles/mri-pulse-sequence-abbreviations
* MR-TIP — structured lists comparing acronyms across vendors: https://mr-tip.com/serv1.php?type=cam and https://mr-tip.com/serv1.php?type=cam&sub=1

<<<code>>>
import sys, os
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.abspath(".."))   # make ../mri_tools.py importable
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import mri_tools as mt
mt.setup_plots()
%matplotlib inline

<<<md>>>
## 1. Glossary

### A

* **Acoustic noise** — the loud knocking/buzzing during a scan. Rapidly switched currents in the gradient coils feel the Lorentz force in $B_0$ and make the coil vibrate like a loudspeaker; levels can exceed 100 dB(A), so hearing protection is mandatory. → [[B4]], [[B6]]
* **Adiabatic pulse** — an RF pulse whose frequency and amplitude are swept slowly enough that $\vec M$ follows the effective field; it gives the same flip (typically inversion) even if $B_1$ varies a lot. → [[J3]]
* **Aliasing (wrap-around)** — parts of the object outside the field of view appear folded into the opposite side of the image, because k-space was sampled too coarsely ($\Delta k > 1/\text{object size}$). In Cartesian MRI it happens in the phase-encode direction. → [[G1]], [[I2]]
* **Analog-to-digital converter (ADC)** — the electronics that turns the received analog voltage into numbers ("samples") at a fixed dwell time. Not to be confused with the *apparent diffusion coefficient*, which has the same acronym. → [[B5]]
* **Angular momentum** — the "amount of rotation" of a spinning object; nuclei with spin carry an intrinsic angular momentum, which is why they precess instead of simply aligning with a field. → [[RP3]]
* **Apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC)** — the diffusion coefficient measured in tissue with diffusion-weighted MRI ("apparent" because cell walls hinder free diffusion). Low ADC indicates e.g. acute stroke. → [[Notation]]
* **Array coil (phased array)** — a receive coil made of many small elements (8–64 "channels"), each with its own receiver. Gives the high SNR of small coils over a large region and enables parallel imaging. → [[B2]], [[B5]]
* **Auto-calibration (ACS lines)** — a small fully sampled region at the centre of k-space acquired within an accelerated scan, used to estimate coil sensitivities or GRAPPA weights. → [[H3]]

### B

* **$B_0$ (main field)** — the strong, static, homogeneous magnetic field of the scanner (0.55–7 T), along $z$. Its job is polarization; it also sets the Larmor frequency. → [[B1]], [[B3]]
* **$B_1$ (RF field)** — the weak (µT) magnetic field rotating at the Larmor frequency produced by the RF coil; $B_1^+$ denotes the transmit field that tips spins, $B_1^-$ the receive sensitivity. → [[B1]], [[E1]]
* **Balanced SSFP (bSSFP)** — a fast gradient-echo sequence in which all gradients are balanced (net zero area) in every TR and nothing is spoiled; very high SNR, $T_2/T_1$ contrast, but dark "banding" where off-resonance is large. → [[H2]]
* **Bandwidth (receiver, readout)** — the range of frequencies sampled during the readout, $BW_{read}=1/\Delta t$; often quoted per pixel. High bandwidth = short readout, less chemical-shift/distortion artifact, but lower SNR. → [[G3]]
* **Birdcage coil** — the usual cylindrical transmit (body or head) coil: two rings joined by "rungs" with capacitors, producing a homogeneous circularly polarized $B_1$ field. → [[B2]], [[B5]]
* **Bloch equation** — the differential equation that describes how $\vec M$ precesses around $\vec B$ and relaxes back to equilibrium; the master equation of MRI. → [[A2]]
* **BOLD (blood-oxygen-level-dependent) contrast** — deoxygenated haemoglobin is paramagnetic and shortens $T_2^*$; brain activity changes blood oxygenation and thus the $T_2^*$-weighted signal. The basis of functional MRI. → [[D2]]
* **Body coil** — the large birdcage RF coil built into the scanner bore, used for transmission (and sometimes reception). → [[B2]]
* **Boltzmann distribution** — the statistical law giving how many spins occupy each energy level at temperature $T$; it predicts the tiny (~10 ppm at 3 T) excess of spins aligned with $B_0$. → [[A1]], [[RP4]]

### C

* **Calibration (prescan)** — automatic measurements before each scan: centre frequency, shimming, RF transmit gain (flip-angle calibration) and receiver gain. → [[B1]]
* **Cartesian sampling** — acquiring k-space on a rectangular grid, line by line (frequency + phase encoding), so that the image is reconstructed with an FFT. → [[F2]], [[G3]]
* **Centre frequency** — the RF frequency set to match the water resonance of the current patient. → [[B1]]
* **Chemical shift** — a small change of resonance frequency caused by the electron cloud around a nucleus shielding it from $B_0$; measured in ppm. Fat protons resonate ≈ 3.5 ppm below water protons. → [[A3]]
* **Chemical-shift displacement artifact** — because MRI maps frequency to position, fat appears shifted relative to water along the readout (or along phase encoding in EPI), giving a bright band on one side and a dark band on the other. → [[I2]]
* **Coil sensitivity ($C_n$)** — how strongly a receive coil element "sees" signal from each position; strongest near the element. → [[B2]], [[H3]]
* **Compressed sensing (CS)** — reconstructing images from pseudo-randomly undersampled k-space by exploiting that images are sparse in some transform domain (e.g. wavelets). → [[H3]], [[J5]]
* **Conjugate symmetry** — the Fourier transform of a real image satisfies $M(-\vec k)=M^*(\vec k)$; partial Fourier imaging exploits this. → [[RM4]], [[H3]]
* **Contrast** — the difference in signal between tissues that lets you tell them apart; controlled by sequence parameters (TE, TR, TI, flip angle, preparation). → [[D1]]
* **Contrast agent** — an injected substance (usually gadolinium-based) that shortens relaxation times where it accumulates. → [[D2]]
* **Convolution** — "smearing" one function with another; multiplication in k-space is convolution in image space (convolution theorem). → [[RM4]], [[I1]]
* **Cross-talk** — imperfect slice profiles partially excite neighbouring slices in 2D multislice imaging, reducing signal; avoided with slice gaps or sharper pulses. → [[H1]]
* **Cryostat / cryogen** — the vacuum-insulated vessel and the liquid helium (4.2 K) that keep the superconducting magnet cold. → [[B3]]

### D

* **Data acquisition (DAQ) window** — the time during which the ADC samples the signal; drawn as a box on pulse-sequence diagrams. → [[C1]]
* **Deep-learning (DL) reconstruction** — neural networks trained on many datasets to reconstruct images from undersampled data (often "unrolled" networks that alternate data consistency and learned denoising). → [[H3]], [[J5]]
* **Demodulation** — removing the ≈ 100 MHz carrier from the received signal (mixing down to baseband) so that only the slow, information-carrying envelope remains; it is what puts us in the rotating frame. → [[B5]], [[C2]]
* **Dephasing** — spins in a voxel precessing at slightly different frequencies fan out in phase, so their sum (the signal) shrinks. → [[D2]], [[D3]]
* **Diamagnetic** — a material that is weakly magnetized *against* an applied field ($\chi<0$); water and most tissue. → [[A3]]
* **Dielectric (RF) shading** — uneven image brightness caused by $B_1^+$ inhomogeneity because the RF wavelength in tissue becomes comparable to the body size at 3 T and above. → [[B5]], [[I2]]
* **Diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI)** — strong gradient pulses make the signal sensitive to random water motion; restricted diffusion appears bright. Usually read out with EPI. → [[Notation]] (not covered in depth in this series)
* **Dixon imaging** — separating water and fat images from echoes acquired at different TEs, using the chemical-shift phase difference. → [[D2]]
* **Dwell time** — time between two successive ADC samples, $\Delta t$; its inverse is the readout bandwidth. → [[G3]]

### E

* **Echo** — a temporary regrowth of signal when previously dephased magnetization is brought back into phase (by gradients = gradient echo, or by an RF refocusing pulse = spin echo). → [[D3]]
* **Echo-planar imaging (EPI)** — acquiring many (or all) k-space lines after a single excitation by rapidly alternating the readout gradient; very fast but prone to distortion and ghosting. → [[H2]]
* **Echo spacing / echo train length (ESP / ETL)** — in FSE and EPI: time between successive echoes, and number of echoes (k-space lines) per excitation. → [[H2]]
* **Eddy currents** — currents induced in conductive scanner structures by switching gradients; they distort the gradient waveforms and must be compensated ("pre-emphasis"). → [[B4]]
* **Encoding matrix ($\mathbf E$)** — the linear model mapping the unknown image to the measured data; reconstruction means inverting it. → [[F3]], [[J5]]
* **Equilibrium magnetization ($M_0$)** — the net magnetization when spins have fully relaxed in $B_0$; proportional to spin density and $B_0$, inversely to temperature. → [[A1]]
* **Ernst angle** — the flip angle that maximizes the signal of a spoiled gradient echo for a given TR and $T_1$, $\theta_E=\arccos(e^{-TR/T_1})$. → [[D1]]
* **Excitation** — tipping the net magnetization away from $B_0$ (into the transverse plane) with an RF pulse at the Larmor frequency. → [[A1]], [[E1]]

### F

* **Faraday cage (RF shield)** — the copper/aluminium shielding of the scan room that keeps external radio signals out (and the scanner's RF in). An open door causes "zipper" artifacts. → [[B5]]
* **Faraday's law of induction** — a changing magnetic flux through a loop induces a voltage; this is how receive coils detect the precessing magnetization. → [[RP2]]
* **Fast (turbo) spin echo (FSE/TSE/RARE)** — many refocusing pulses after one excitation, each echo filling a different k-space line; the clinical workhorse for $T_2$ and PD weighting. → [[H2]]
* **Fat suppression** — removing fat signal by spectrally selective saturation/inversion, inversion recovery (STIR), or Dixon separation. → [[D2]]
* **Ferromagnetic** — strongly magnetic materials (iron, nickel, cobalt, many steels) that are pulled violently into the magnet — the "projectile" hazard. → [[A3]], [[B6]]
* **Field of view (FOV)** — the size of the imaged region, $FOV=1/\Delta k$. → [[G1]]
* **Flip angle ($\theta$)** — the angle by which an RF pulse rotates the magnetization. → [[C1]], [[E1]]
* **Free induction decay (FID)** — the decaying signal right after an excitation, without any refocusing; it decays with $T_2^*$. → [[A2]]
* **FLAIR** — fluid-attenuated inversion recovery: an inversion time chosen to null CSF, so lesions near ventricles stand out on $T_2$-weighted images. → [[D4]]
* **Fourier transform** — the mathematical tool that decomposes a signal into frequencies; MRI data are (samples of) the Fourier transform of the image. → [[RM3]], [[C2]]
* **Frequency encoding** — turning on a gradient during readout so that the precession frequency encodes position along one axis. → [[F1]]
* **Fringe field** — the magnetic field that extends outside the magnet; the 0.5 mT (5 gauss) line marks the area restricted for pacemaker wearers. → [[B3]], [[B6]]

### G

* **g-factor** — "geometry factor" ($g\ge1$): how much parallel imaging amplifies noise at each position, on top of the $\sqrt R$ loss from shorter scanning. → [[H3]]
* **Gadolinium-based contrast agent (GBCA)** — chelated Gd³⁺, a strongly paramagnetic ion that mainly shortens $T_1$ (bright on $T_1$-weighted images). → [[D2]], [[B6]]
* **Ghosting** — faint shifted copies of the object along the phase-encode direction, caused by periodic motion, flow, or (in EPI) by differences between odd and even echoes ("Nyquist" or N/2 ghost). → [[I2]], [[H2]]
* **Gibbs ringing (truncation artifact)** — ripples next to sharp edges because k-space is sampled only up to $k_{max}$; reduced by higher resolution or k-space windowing. → [[I1]], [[I2]]
* **Gradient (field gradient, gradient coil)** — a coil that makes $B_z$ vary linearly with position ($B_z=B_0+\vec G\cdot\vec r$), used for slice selection and spatial encoding. → [[B1]], [[B4]]
* **Gradient echo (GRE, GE)** — an echo formed by reversing a gradient; does not refocus off-resonance, so it is $T_2^*$-weighted. → [[D3]]
* **Gradient non-linearity** — real gradient fields deviate from linear far from isocentre, warping images; corrected by "distortion correction". → [[I2]], [[B4]]
* **GRAPPA** — a k-space parallel imaging method that fills in skipped lines as weighted combinations of acquired neighbouring lines from all coils, with weights learned from auto-calibration lines. → [[H3]], [[J5]]
* **Gyromagnetic ratio ($\gamma$, $\bar\gamma$)** — the constant linking field strength to precession frequency for a nucleus ($\bar\gamma=42.58$ MHz/T for ¹H). → [[A1]]

### H–I

* **Hard pulse** — a short rectangular RF pulse; not frequency-selective. → [[E1]]
* **Homogeneity** — how uniform $B_0$ is over the imaging volume, quoted in ppm over a sphere of given diameter. → [[B3]]
* **Image reconstruction** — turning raw k-space data into an image (an inverse FFT for fully sampled Cartesian data; iterative optimization for accelerated data). → [[F3]]
* **In-phase / out-of-phase (opposed-phase) imaging** — gradient echoes at TEs where fat and water signals add or subtract; used for Dixon and to detect fat within a voxel. → [[D2]]
* **Inversion recovery (IR)** — a 180° pulse flips $M_z$ to $-M_0$; imaging after an inversion time TI gives strong $T_1$ contrast or nulls a chosen tissue. → [[D1]]
* **Isocentre** — the centre of the magnet, where gradient fields are zero and the field is most homogeneous. → [[B2]]
* **Isochromat** — a group of spins experiencing the same field and hence precessing at the same frequency; the unit of Bloch simulations. → [[A2]]

### K–L

* **k-space** — the spatial-frequency domain in which MRI data are acquired; the centre holds contrast and overall signal, the edges hold fine detail. → [[F2]], [[RM6]]
* **k-space trajectory** — the path through k-space traced by the gradients ($\vec k(t)=\bar\gamma\int\vec G\,dt$): Cartesian lines, EPI zig-zag, radial spokes, spirals. → [[F2]]
* **Lab frame** — the coordinate system fixed to the scanner, in which spins precess at the Larmor frequency. → [[A2]]
* **Larmor frequency** — the precession (resonance) frequency $f_0=\bar\gamma B_0$: 63.9 MHz at 1.5 T, 127.7 MHz at 3 T. → [[A1]]
* **Longitudinal magnetization ($M_z$)** — the component of $\vec M$ along $B_0$; not directly detected, but it is the "reservoir" for the next excitation. → [[A1]]
* **Low-noise preamplifier (LNA)** — the first amplifier right at each receive coil element; its noise figure (< 0.5 dB) determines how much noise the electronics add. → [[B5]]

### M

* **Magnetic moment ($\vec\mu$)** — the "strength and direction" of a tiny magnet; each ¹H nucleus has one. → [[A1]]
* **Magnetic susceptibility ($\chi$)** — how strongly a material becomes magnetized in a field; differences between tissues and air distort $B_0$. → [[A3]]
* **Magnetization preparation** — an RF/gradient module before the imaging readout (inversion, saturation, $T_2$-prep, fat-sat, diffusion) that encodes extra contrast or suppresses a tissue. → [[H2]]
* **Magnetization transfer (MT)** — exchange of saturation between water and protons bound to macromolecules; produces contrast and is a side effect of many off-resonant RF pulses. → [[D2]] (mentioned only)
* **Matrix size** — the number of pixels in each direction ($N_x\times N_y$), equal to the number of k-space samples per direction for Cartesian imaging. → [[G1]]
* **Motion artifact** — blurring and ghosting caused by the patient (or blood, or the heart) moving during the seconds-to-minutes of k-space acquisition. → [[I2]]
* **MPRAGE** — magnetization-prepared rapid gradient echo: an inversion pulse followed by a train of fast spoiled gradient echoes; the standard 3D $T_1$-weighted brain scan. → [[H2]]
* **MR Safe / MR Conditional / MR Unsafe** — labels for implants and equipment: safe in any MR environment / safe only under stated conditions (field, SAR, spatial gradient) / must never enter. → [[B6]]
* **Multislice (2D) imaging** — acquiring several slices interleaved within one TR by changing the RF frequency. → [[H1]]

### N–O

* **Net magnetization ($\vec M$)** — the vector sum of all nuclear moments per unit volume; the quantity MRI actually measures. → [[A1]]
* **NEX / NSA / averages** — number of times each k-space line is acquired and averaged; SNR grows as $\sqrt{NEX}$, time as $NEX$. → [[G2]]
* **Noise (thermal)** — random voltage from thermal motion of charges, mostly in the patient's conductive tissue; zero-mean Gaussian in the raw data. → [[G2]], [[B5]]
* **Nyquist criterion** — to avoid aliasing you must sample at least twice the highest frequency (in MRI: $\Delta k\le1/\text{object size}$). → [[RM5]], [[G1]]
* **Off-resonance** — spins precessing at a frequency different from the RF/reference frequency, due to field inhomogeneity, susceptibility, or chemical shift. → [[D2]]

### P

* **Parallel imaging (PI)** — using the different spatial sensitivities of array-coil elements to replace skipped k-space lines (SENSE, GRAPPA) or to separate simultaneously excited slices (SMS). → [[H3]]
* **Paramagnetic** — weakly magnetized *along* an applied field ($\chi>0$): oxygen, deoxyhaemoglobin, gadolinium. → [[A3]]
* **Partial Fourier** — acquiring only a little more than half of k-space and using conjugate symmetry to fill in the rest. → [[H3]]
* **Partial volume** — a voxel containing a mixture of tissues; its signal is a (complex!) average, which can cancel at fat–water boundaries. → [[I2]]
* **Peripheral nerve stimulation (PNS)** — tingling or twitching caused by electric fields induced by fast gradient switching (large $dB/dt$); limits slew rate. → [[B4]], [[B6]]
* **Phase** — the angle of the transverse magnetization in the $x$–$y$ plane; MRI encodes position, flow and susceptibility into phase. → [[A2]], [[RM2]]
* **Phase encoding** — applying a gradient pulse *before* readout, with a different area each TR, so that position along a second axis is encoded in phase. → [[F1]]
* **Point spread function (PSF)** — the image of a single point; describes the blurring and ringing of an acquisition. → [[I1]]
* **Polarization** — the slight preferential alignment of spins with $B_0$ that creates the net magnetization. → [[A1]]
* **Precession** — the rotation of a magnetic moment (or $\vec M$) around the magnetic field, like a tilted spinning top around gravity. → [[A1]], [[RP3]]
* **Proton density (PD)** — the density of *MR-visible* (mobile) hydrogen nuclei; PD-weighted images minimize $T_1$ and $T_2$ effects (long TR, short TE). → [[D1]]
* **Pulse sequence** — the timed recipe of RF pulses, gradients and data-acquisition windows that defines an MRI experiment. → [[C1]]

### Q–R

* **Quality factor (Q)** — how sharp the resonance of a coil is ($Q=\omega L/R$); the ratio of unloaded to loaded Q tells whether noise is dominated by the patient (desirable) or by the coil. → [[B5]]
* **Quench** — the sudden loss of superconductivity in the magnet: the stored energy boils off the liquid helium, which must be vented outside through a quench pipe. → [[B3]], [[B6]]
* **Radiofrequency (RF)** — electromagnetic oscillations in the ~3 kHz–300 GHz range; MRI uses ≈ 20–300 MHz (the FM-radio/VHF band at 1.5–3 T). → [[B1]]
* **Readout** — the part of a sequence during which data are sampled with the frequency-encoding gradient on. → [[F1]]
* **Receive chain** — coil element → preamplifier → (mixing/filtering) → ADC → digital demodulation → reconstruction computer. → [[B5]]
* **Reciprocity** — the receive sensitivity of a coil at a point equals the field it would produce there per unit current; links $B_1^-$ to coil geometry. → [[B2]], [[RP2]]
* **Refocusing pulse** — a 180° RF pulse that reverses the phase accumulated from off-resonance, so spins realign at the spin echo. → [[D3]]
* **Relaxation** — the return of $\vec M$ to equilibrium: $M_z$ recovers with $T_1$, $M_{xy}$ decays with $T_2$ (or $T_2^*$). → [[A1]], [[A2]]
* **Repetition time (TR)** — time between successive excitations; controls $T_1$ weighting and scan time. → [[C1]]
* **Resolution (spatial)** — the size of the smallest distinguishable detail, set by $\delta=1/(2k_{max})$. → [[G1]]
* **Resonance** — a strong response when a system is driven at its natural frequency; spins absorb RF energy only near the Larmor frequency. → [[A1]], [[RP2]]
* **Rotating frame** — a coordinate system rotating with the RF at ≈ the Larmor frequency; in it the RF field appears static and only off-resonance remains as precession. → [[A2]]

### S

* **SAR (specific absorption rate)** — RF power deposited per kg of tissue (W/kg); scales roughly with $B_0^2$ and flip angle², and is limited by safety standards. → [[E1]], [[B6]]
* **SENSE** — an image-domain parallel imaging method that "unfolds" aliased pixels using measured coil-sensitivity maps. → [[H3]], [[J5]]
* **Shimming** — making $B_0$ more homogeneous, passively (iron pieces at installation) and actively (shim coils, adjusted for each patient). → [[B3]]
* **Signal equation** — the formula $s(t)=\int m(\vec r)e^{-i2\pi\vec k(t)\cdot\vec r}d\vec r$ stating that MRI data are samples of the Fourier transform of the image. → [[C2]]
* **Signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)** — signal strength relative to noise; $SNR\propto$ voxel volume × $\sqrt{\text{sampling time}}$ × sequence factor. → [[G2]]
* **Simultaneous multislice (SMS, multiband)** — exciting several slices at once with a multiband RF pulse and separating them with coil sensitivities. → [[H3]]
* **Sinc pulse** — an RF pulse shaped like $\sin(\pi x)/(\pi x)$, whose spectrum is approximately a rectangle, giving a well-defined slice. → [[E1]], [[E2]]
* **Slew rate** — how fast a gradient can change ($dG/dt$, T/m/s); limited by amplifier voltage, coil inductance and nerve stimulation. → [[B4]]
* **Slice selection** — exciting only a thin slab by applying a frequency-selective RF pulse while a gradient is on. → [[E2]]
* **Spin** — an intrinsic quantum property of particles and nuclei giving them angular momentum and a magnetic moment; "spins" in MRI slang means the ¹H nuclei. → [[A1]], [[RP4]]
* **Spin echo (SE)** — the echo formed by a 180° refocusing pulse, which cancels static off-resonance; gives $T_2$ (not $T_2^*$) weighting. → [[D3]]
* **Spoiling** — destroying leftover transverse magnetization before the next excitation, by gradient "crushers" and/or RF phase cycling (RF spoiling). → [[H2]]
* **Spoiled gradient echo (SPGR/FLASH)** — a fast GRE with spoiling every TR; pure $T_1$ weighting at moderate flip angles. → [[D1]], [[H2]]
* **Steady state** — after many TRs, the magnetization before each pulse becomes the same every TR; fast sequences operate in it. → [[D1]], [[H2]]
* **STIR** — short-TI inversion recovery: TI chosen to null fat (≈ 150–250 ms depending on field); robust fat suppression. → [[D1]]
* **Sum of squares (SoS)** — combining array-coil images by taking the square root of the sum of squared magnitudes. → [[B2]]
* **Superconducting magnet** — a solenoid of niobium–titanium wire cooled below its critical temperature, carrying current with zero resistance (persistent mode). → [[B3]]
* **Susceptibility artifact** — signal loss and geometric distortion near air–tissue interfaces or metal caused by local $B_0$ distortions. → [[I2]]

### T

* **$T_1$ (longitudinal / spin–lattice relaxation time)** — time constant of $M_z$ recovery toward $M_0$. → [[A1]], [[D1]]
* **$T_2$ (transverse / spin–spin relaxation time)** — time constant of irreversible $M_{xy}$ decay from spin–spin interactions. → [[A1]], [[D1]]
* **$T_2^*$** — effective transverse decay time including dephasing from field inhomogeneity within a voxel; $T_2^*\le T_2$; refocusable by a spin echo. → [[D2]]
* **TE (echo time)** — time from excitation to the echo (centre of k-space); controls $T_2$/$T_2^*$ weighting. → [[C1]]
* **TI (inversion time)** — time from inversion pulse to excitation. → [[D1]]
* **Time–bandwidth product (TBW)** — pulse duration × bandwidth; a property of the pulse shape that sets how sharp the slice profile is. → [[E1]]
* **Transverse magnetization ($M_{xy}$)** — the component of $\vec M$ perpendicular to $B_0$; the only part that produces a detectable signal. → [[A2]]
* **Tuning and matching** — adjusting a coil's capacitors so it resonates at the Larmor frequency and presents 50 Ω to the cable/amplifier. → [[B5]]

### U–Z

* **Undersampling** — acquiring fewer k-space samples than Nyquist requires, to save time; needs special reconstruction. → [[H3]]
* **Voxel** — volume element: the 3D box (pixel × slice thickness) represented by one image value. → [[G2]]
* **Water–fat shift** — the fat–water chemical-shift displacement expressed in pixels (Philips specifies readout bandwidth this way). → [[I2]]
* **Windowing (apodization)** — tapering k-space data toward the edges to reduce Gibbs ringing, at the cost of slightly lower resolution. → [[I1]]
* **Zero-filling** — padding k-space with zeros before the FFT to interpolate the image to a finer grid (does not add real resolution). → [[G2]]
* **Zipper artifact** — a line of noise at one readout position caused by external RF interference (e.g. an open door). → [[I2]]

<<<md>>>
## 2. Acronym table

| Acronym | Stands for | In one line | Chapter |
|---|---|---|---|
| ACS | auto-calibration signal | fully sampled k-space centre used to calibrate PI | [[H3]] |
| ADC | analog-to-digital converter **or** apparent diffusion coefficient | context! | [[B5]] |
| ARC | Autocalibrating Reconstruction for Cartesian imaging | GE's GRAPPA-type PI | [[H3]] |
| ASL | arterial spin labeling | perfusion imaging with magnetically labelled blood | — |
| ASSET | Array coil Spatial Sensitivity Encoding Technique | GE's SENSE-type PI | [[H3]] |
| BOLD | blood-oxygen-level-dependent | $T_2^*$ contrast for fMRI | [[D2]] |
| bSSFP | balanced steady-state free precession | fully balanced fast GRE | [[H2]] |
| BW, RBW | (receiver) bandwidth | $1/\Delta t$ | [[G3]] |
| CAIPIRINHA | Controlled Aliasing In Parallel Imaging Results IN Higher Acceleration | shifts aliased slices/lines for better PI | [[H3]] |
| CNR | contrast-to-noise ratio | difference of signals / noise | [[G2]] |
| CPMG | Carr–Purcell–Meiboom–Gill | refocusing-pulse train with phase that makes FSE robust | [[H2]] |
| CS | compressed sensing | sparsity-based reconstruction | [[H3]] |
| CSF | cerebrospinal fluid | long $T_1$, long $T_2$ | [[D1]] |
| DAQ | data acquisition | ADC sampling window | [[C1]] |
| DCE / DSC | dynamic contrast-enhanced / dynamic susceptibility contrast | perfusion with Gd bolus ($T_1$ / $T_2^*$) | — |
| DICOM | Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine | standard image file/network format | [[Software]] |
| DL | deep learning | learned reconstruction | [[H3]] |
| DTI / DWI | diffusion tensor / diffusion-weighted imaging | diffusion contrast | — |
| EPI | echo-planar imaging | many lines per excitation | [[H2]] |
| ESP, $t_{esp}$ | echo spacing | time between echoes | [[H2]] |
| ETL | echo train length | echoes per excitation | [[H2]] |
| FE, RO | frequency encoding, readout | | [[F1]] |
| FFE | fast field echo | Philips name for gradient echo | (vendor table) |
| FID | free induction decay | signal right after excitation | [[A2]] |
| FLAIR | fluid-attenuated inversion recovery | CSF-nulled IR | [[D4]] |
| FLASH | fast low-angle shot | Siemens spoiled GRE | [[H2]] |
| fMRI | functional MRI | brain activation via BOLD | [[D2]] |
| FOV | field of view | $1/\Delta k$ | [[G1]] |
| FSE / TSE | fast / turbo spin echo | multi-echo SE (= RARE) | [[H2]] |
| FWHM | full width at half maximum | width of a PSF | [[I1]] |
| GBCA | gadolinium-based contrast agent | | [[D2]] |
| GE / GRE | gradient echo / gradient-recalled echo (also: the vendor GE) | | [[D3]] |
| GRAPPA | GeneRalized Autocalibrating Partially Parallel Acquisitions | k-space PI | [[H3]] |
| HASTE | Half-Fourier Acquisition Single-shot Turbo spin Echo | single-shot FSE | [[H2]] |
| IEC | International Electrotechnical Commission | IEC 60601-2-33 sets MR safety limits | [[B6]] |
